{
 "title": "Saanjh Ki Baansuri (The Flute at Dusk)",
 "concept": "Saanjh Ki Baansuri: two lovers as two shades of Ma. The bansuri's seed, heard as the seal glows, rises through tivra Ma: Ni Re Ga, Ma# Dha ~Pa. The violin answers falling through shuddha Ma: Dha Pa Ma Ga Re. The groom's flute climbs to tar Sa on his name; the bride's violin replies in a Pahadi pentatonic and blooms on Dha on hers. At the floral ring the seed grows into the full mukhda (Ga Ma# Dha Pa Ga, Re Ga Pa Dha Sa', Ni Dha Pa) over a descending bass, the violin echoing and then singing in thirds. As the curtains close, bVI-bVII lifts into the grand tutti, where the answer becomes the cadence. The card hears the mukhda at half speed over a D/F# Yaman glow; the finale sings it again, holds Sa' over bVI, and settles on C6/9 with a swarmandal and santoor flourish.",
 "meter": "12/8",
 "key": "C major (Sa = C), with borrowed bVI (Ab) and bVII (Bb)",
 "mode_notes": "Yaman Kalyan colour over C major: tivra Ma F# on rising phrases (Ni Re Ga, Ga Ma# Dha, Ma# Dha Pa), shuddha Ma F on falling phrases (Pa Ma Ga Re); Ni (B) leans to Sa'. Bride's phrases are Pahadi-pentatonic (Sa Re Ga Pa Dha). Komal Ga (Eb), komal Dha (Ab) and komal Ni (Bb) appear only in the bVI-bVII lifts at 28.5-30 and 55.5-57. The card uses D/F# as the Yaman II chord.",
